<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Apple Privacy Manifest.

Started as the minimum template for .NET MAUI (the APIs the framework itself uses).
Two things have been added on top of it, and neither is optional:

  1. NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults. The template ships this block
     COMMENTED OUT with a note saying to enable it if the app uses Preferences.
     This app uses MAUI Preferences in six files (the reminder last-seen marker, the
     boot-recovery flag, per-device pet pause, settings flags, the analytics identity,
     sync cursors), and MAUI Preferences IS NSUserDefaults on this platform. Leaving it
     out fails at UPLOAD with ITMS-91053 ("Missing API declaration"), not at review, so
     it blocks TestFlight rather than release.

  2. NSPrivacyTracking / NSPrivacyTrackingDomains. Declared explicitly rather than
     omitted, because on iOS this app does no tracking at all: Meta ad attribution is
     Android-only (Felova.Meta.Binding wraps com.facebook.android:facebook-core, and iOS
     registers NullAdAttributionService), so no advertising identifier is read and no
     ATT prompt is shown. That is a real difference from the Play listing, where the
     advertising ID IS collected and shared. The two stores' answers must not be copied
     across. See AI/analytics.md -> "The advertising boundary".

     Declaring tracking true with no ATT prompt is a rejection; shipping an ATT prompt
     with nothing to track is worse. Both stay as they are here until Meta's iOS SDK is
     actually bound.

NOT declared here: NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes. That is a deliberate hand-off, not an
oversight. What this app collects is decided at RUNTIME by the owner (with cloud backup
off, which is the default, nothing leaves the device at all), and the authoritative
declaration surface for a first-party app is the App Privacy questionnaire in App Store
Connect, which has to agree with the privacy policy at felova.app/privacy. Filling this
array in from a guess would create a third answer that has to be kept in step with the
other two.

The mapping to use when answering the questionnaire, so the reasoning is not re-derived:
  - Anonymous analytics -> "Product Interaction" (Usage Data), NOT linked to identity,
    NOT used for tracking. The identity is a random GUID (AnalyticsIdentity.cs) and no
    property may ever carry a name, note, or medical detail.
  - Cloud account (opt-in only) -> "Email Address", linked to identity, not used for
    tracking.
  - Backed-up pet records (opt-in only) -> "Other User Content", linked to identity,
    not used for tracking. These are animal records, not the user's own health data:
    HealthKit is not involved anywhere in this app.
See docs/history/IOS_PLAN.md Phase 5.2.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>35F9.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>E174.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!-- CA92.1: "read and write information that is only accessible to the app
             itself". Exactly what MAUI Preferences is used for here; nothing written
             through it is shared with, or readable by, anything outside the app. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
